@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* Alert's Overview, written by hand after the selection and form field Overviews: status and
   variants, dismissing (previewed in each mode), an alert a post renders, and its labels.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<TextBlock>An alert is a message on the page, next to what it's about. It suits the result of a form, or a warning above a table.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>For a message about the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/banner">Banner</Anchor>. For a short notice that doesn't need a place on the page, show a toast from the <Anchor Href="docs/components/toast-region">ToastRegion</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Status</Heading>
<TextBlock>The status is the kind of message: neutral, info, success, warning or danger. It sets the color, the icon, and the word screen readers hear before the message. Set <Code>Status</Code> to choose one.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-usage" />
<TextBlock>To show a different icon, set <Code>Icon</Code>. To show none, set <Code>HideIcon</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Variants</Heading>
<TextBlock>The variant sets how strongly the alert stands out from the page. <Code>Soft</Code> (the default) tints the background, <Code>Outline</Code> shows a border in the status color, and <Code>Solid</Code> fills it with the status color. Set <Code>Variant</Code> to choose one.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-variants" />

<Heading Level="2">Actions and dismissing</Heading>
<TextBlock>A dismissible alert has a close button, so the user can clear a message once it's read. Set <Code>Dismissible</Code> to add it; it works <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor> too. To put buttons or links under the message, add an <Code>AlertActions</Code> at the end of the alert's content.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-dismiss" />
<TextBlock>A closed alert stays closed only while the user stays on the page. It shows again:</TextBlock>
<List>
    <ListItem>after a reload, or on returning to the page;</ListItem>
    <ListItem>after any form post, with JavaScript off;</ListItem>
    <ListItem>after a form post on static pages, if the alert is inside the form that posted or its text has changed;</ListItem>
    <ListItem>on interactive pages, when <Code>Dismissed</Code> is set back to false.</ListItem>
</List>
<TextBlock>So an alert that reports a form post should be inside that form, or its text should say what happened ("Deleted Invoice 12"). Then the next form post shows it again. To know in C# when the user closes the alert on interactive pages, set <Code>@@bind-Dismissed</Code>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>To keep a message closed on every page, use a Banner, which saves the choice in a cookie.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">After a form post</Heading>
<TextBlock>An alert can report what a form post did, such as "Saved". It suits forms whose result belongs next to the form, rather than in a toast. Render the alert from the submit handler; it arrives with the response, so it works in every render mode.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-saved" />
<TextBlock>Put the saved value in the alert's text when you save, rather than binding the text to the field. On interactive pages the field changes as the user types, and the alert would change with it.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, an alert the user has closed stays closed until <Code>Dismissed</Code> changes. This example binds <Code>Dismissed</Code> and sets it to false on each save, so every save shows the alert. On static pages the alert is inside the form that posted, so each response shows it again.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>Screen readers hear a word for the status before the message, and the close button has a name. The defaults are below; to set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>StatusLabel</Code></td><td>The word before the message. Set it to <Code>""</Code> to leave it out.</td><td>"Info", "Success", "Warning" or "Error"; none for <Code>Neutral</Code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>DismissLabel</Code></td><td>The close button's name</td><td>"Dismiss"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;
}
